                <pb facs="0449" n="191" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg191"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1175</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-05-25T17:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">May 25, 1915, 5 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2159. Your 1603, May 23 [22]. Sir Edward
                        Grey informs me that the British Government clearly understand
                    that the cotton arrangement is private and unofficial and in no way involves our
                    Government. He will forthwith give to the newspapers here a statement to this
                    effect and he is so telegraphing to Spring
                        Rice.</p>
                <p>I have exactly followed your instructions given in several telegrams about
                    shipping controversies distinctly to inform the Foreign Office that the
                    representations made must be understood as in no way prejudicial to the American
                    attitude toward the order in council. In conversation also with Sir Edward Grey I have a number of times
                    definitely informed him to the same effect. There is therefore no
                    misunderstanding in his mind on this point.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
